#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2048c5b4-3996-5c47-9a17-90d26d347fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278889} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef2145c-b1f4-56c4-9959-10d10c087ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279000} {:answer/id #uuid "762200d2-8ac3-54b0-a844-d5ae31f4789b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279645} {:answer/id #uuid "dddcd65b-9ce6-50ce-9228-6bcf5cea7db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280548} {:answer/id #uuid "e26ca2e7-67dd-5dc7-a4ef-404b70019c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dddcd65b-9ce6-50ce-9228-6bcf5cea7db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280548} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eae0065-06b1-55e5-93fa-33a43fe9d0d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff69a5c1-505a-5719-b6b9-7ec69e0931ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-162664" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a{x}^{2},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- There is a function $f(x)\\in Q,$ such that $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- For any $f(x)\\in Q$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in Q$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider the null function $f(x)=0=0{x}^{2}\\in Q.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot f(x)=(-1)\\cdot 0=0=f(x).\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any function $f(x)\\in Q$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(x),g(x)\\in Q$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e69ab93-723d-5565-8b12-c9c9e92f4afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342360} {:answer/id #uuid "1766df50-f341-5eb6-89ac-0097e98a3fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342444} {:answer/id #uuid "6815ad43-217d-52e9-a8da-0aa78e231e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343124} {:answer/id #uuid "ca897927-fa4a-50ee-97a5-f96cb800a091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344018} {:answer/id #uuid "f42d0555-d3d6-510b-a3a2-94555e1c8df5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca897927-fa4a-50ee-97a5-f96cb800a091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344018} :answer-field/id #uuid "840022d0-492a-56fe-bf5a-c6203cea1ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73f62269-52b9-5341-87d2-165ee03b0821" :question/math-academy-id "q-74700" :question/problem "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero-polynomial belongs to $U.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n- For any $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in U,$ we have $[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x)=p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as \n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot {x}^{3}.\n$$\n\n \nHence, $0\\in U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider polynomials $p(x)={x}^{3},$ $q(x)=2{x}^{3},$ and $r(x)=3{x}^{3}$ from $U.$ Then: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x) & =[{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}]-3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\ p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)] & ={x}^{3}-[2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}] \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-[-{x}^{3}] \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x)\\ne p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e019c0c-c668-5ed1-a867-43f40e187002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358011} {:answer/id #uuid "93241e3f-6a8f-5ad8-bfac-2641dcb5f85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359039} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1ff7cd-a746-57e7-b6af-1dcad05500df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359224} {:answer/id #uuid "ba745c6e-4d9f-526e-b1ed-68947cdeb6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359373} {:answer/id #uuid "d0fa2cf0-d079-54dc-a60a-17daf11e5c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e019c0c-c668-5ed1-a867-43f40e187002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358011} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fea600c-8657-5d8b-912c-ccccb2894d70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d30a6a74-1882-58de-a0e8-caf74485928f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94700" :question/problem "Let $R$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in R,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n- For any $A\\in R,$ we have $-1(-1\\cdot A)=-A.$\n- For any $A\\in R$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R$ with real entries, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {a}_{ij}={a}_{ij}\n$$\n\nand, hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $R.$  Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1(-1\\cdot A) & =-1(-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-1([\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot 1 & (-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-1) & (-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\ne -A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
